      "content": "thanks, that's exactly the kind of categorization i was looking for. it's super helpful to see how those different 'strands' actually function as political tools. \n\none thing though—i've seen some older academic papers from the 80s and 90s that actually try to use math and data to show how union density might have led to stagflation or slowed down industrial modernization. i'm worried that if i just dismiss them as 'reactionary rhetoric' in my thesis, a professor might call me out for not being objective. is there any way to address those specific quantitative claims without accidentally giving legitimacy to the idea that unions were actually an economic burden?",
